    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Almost everything is read as written, but with rules.",
     "texto": [
      "In German almost every letter is pronounced. What changes are some combinations.",
      "**ei** sounds like «eye» (heiße) and **ie** sounds like a long «ee» (Sie, wie). **eu** and **äu** sound like «oy» (Freund).",
      "**sch** sounds like «sh» (tschüss). **ch** is soft after e and i (ich), and hard after a, o and u (auch). The hard one is like the «ch» in Scottish «loch».",
      "**w** sounds like a v (wie), not like English w. **v** almost always sounds like «f» (vier). **z** sounds like «ts» (zehn). **j** sounds like «y» (ja).",
      "The **s** at the start of a word, before a vowel, buzzes like the «z» in «zoo» (Sie, sehr)."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
